    "q-95568": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95568",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sech}⁡3x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=4\\operatorname{sech}⁡g,\\,g=3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=-4\\operatorname{sech}⁡g\\operatorname{tanh}⁡g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-4\\operatorname{sech}⁡g\\operatorname{tanh}⁡g\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sech}⁡g\\operatorname{tanh}⁡g \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sech}⁡3x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
