    "q-95892": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95892",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "$$\n\nIf $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ then ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{g(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}.$ What is $g(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\overset{g(x)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
